        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"rostand-cyrano-de-bergerac-5e7e790fa3b03be7\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"Roxane directly avows that she loves Cyrano, immediately using that avowal to support her plea that he remain alive. The love clause presents her emotional state as true. It does not independently undertake a commitment, and the urgency of the scene does not by itself establish expressive/reflexive force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The compressed, urgent delivery is highly emotional, but there is no positive evidence that the words are a reflex-like discharge rather than an intense avowal.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Roxane directly addresses the mortally wounded Cyrano: “Je vous aime, vivez !”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Having discovered that Cyrano was the true authorial and emotional presence behind Christian’s letters, Roxane recognizes him as the single beloved she had attributed to another man. Her declaration is sincere and functions rhetorically as the premise for an urgent plea that he live. Cyrano’s fairy-tale response treats the words as potentially redemptive or transformative, but this effect does not make the core love avowal a relational undertaking or an expressive reflex.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Roxane says directly: “Je vous aime, vivez !”\",\"supports\":\"The present-tense first-person declaration strongly supports truth-conditional avowal; “vivez” supplies the urgent life-or-death context.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand, Roxane learns that Cyrano is dying and has recognized that the tears and words in Christian’s letter were Cyrano’s.\",\"supports\":\"Her declaration follows a discovery about Cyrano’s long-concealed love and authorship, supporting a sincere recognition and avowal rather than a conventional token.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Roxane later says, “Je n'aimais qu'un seul être et je le perds deux fois !”\",\"supports\":\"This clarifies that she now understands Christian and Cyrano as the two apparent embodiments of the one person she loved, reinforcing the truth-conditional reading.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Cyrano answers that only in fairy tales does saying “Je t'aime !” make the ashamed prince’s ugliness melt.\",\"supports\":\"His response receives her words as a love declaration with imagined redemptive power, while denying that they can alter his condition or self-image.\",\"confidence\":0.95}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core as a direct avowal. The attached imperative and tragic intensity are adequately handled as context and do not create residual pressure for P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
